**Repeatable motifs / props.** Suspended metal catwalk slabs; bright nodes on gridded vertical walls; fine and heavy black wiring in layers; dark upright tabletop telephone mechanism with crimson pool at ~02:46; sparse glass/metal lamps; small floating cylinders/spools in the middle acts; round ventilation fan embedded in back wall of tiled room; warm pipe tangle at the climax. Keep scale and material family consistent when a recurring object reappears. Do not add familiar real-world phones, futuristic displays, modern clothing, elephants, or natural landscapes merely because of the title.

## Handoff and checks

1. DP picks the matching source reference from `design-reference-v1` only to study character/location/layout. Paint/render the shot anew and record the source time, shot ID, artwork version and any simplification in its log. No original frame may be a pixel source in a delivered shot.
2. Preserve the identity anchors in medium/close frames: Proog's bald angular grey-fringed head + olive coat + maroon chest, Emo's short dark hair + slender neck/arms + light fitted shirt. Wide frames retain color-blocking and two contrasting silhouettes. Make the original action/framing legible; a camera pan over an empty texture does not fulfil an interaction shot.
3. QC compares matching source time and the **new** cut shot for subject, action, setting, color family and character continuity, in addition to director's D3. Report precise cut timecodes; use this sheet as the consistency standard. Intentional creative deviations require director assent.
4. All times here identify source survey frames; director's forthcoming `direction/shotlist.*` will supply authoritative shot boundaries/IDs. The separate shot-level map follows that list rather than guessing cuts. The source film was sampled at 15-second intervals and selected full-resolution instants; unseen frames may reveal further props or color changes. Log discoveries and revise this sheet rather than forcing wrong details across shots.

